+# Helpdesk API
+
+Rails 8.1, API-only, PostgreSQL, Puma. There is no server-rendered frontend,
+cookie session, or additional service required for development. All business
+endpoints are JSON under `/api/v1`.
+
+## Email support (Action Mailbox)
+
+Set `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` and `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD`, run
+`bin/rails db:prepare`, and configure your mail server to relay mail for the
+**whole support domain**, including private reply addresses. For the default
+domain, Acme's public address is `acme@support.example.test`.
+
+The native Rails relay accepts RFC822 messages at:
+
+```sh
+curl --user "actionmailbox:$RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD" \
+  --header 'Content-Type: message/rfc822' \
+  --data-binary @message.eml \
+  https://api.example.test/rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+```
+
+Use **HTTPS** and a strong ingress password. The ingress does not require portal
+headers or a bearer token. Correctly authenticated deliveries receive **204**,
+including mail intentionally ignored by business rules; invalid credentials
+receive **401**, and a wrong content type receives **415**.
+
+* To, Cc, and available Bcc recipients identify the business. Non-support
+  recipients are ignored; mail addressed to multiple businesses or multiple
+  private ticket addresses is ignored rather than copied between tenants.
+* A message to the plain business address opens a new, open ticket with its
+  subject and decoded plain-text body as a public comment. Subject/reply headers
+  alone never thread a new message. Multipart mail uses its plain-text part;
+  HTML-only, attachment-only, blank, automated, and list mail is ignored.
+* Existing business-scoped customers are reused. New customers get an unknown,
+  random password and a normal portal verification email. Receiving mail never
+  verifies an account or resets an existing verification token. After verifying,
+  an email-created customer can use password recovery to choose a password and
+  sign in to the portal. Staff identities, suspended customers, reserved
+  invitation emails, and cancelled businesses cannot create email tickets.
+* Public staff comments on **email-origin** tickets are emailed to the requester.
+  Internal notes, merge notes (even public ones), customer comments, and portal-only tickets send no notification.
+  Each ticket has a random, private `reply+TOKEN@SUPPORT_DOMAIN` address in the
+  notification's `Reply-To`. Replying to that address adds a public comment and
+  reopens pending/solved tickets. Closed tickets reject incoming replies without
+  reopening or forwarding them. Only the requester's normalized From address is
+  accepted; `Reply-To` cannot override it. Reply-all may also include the same
+  business's plain address. Private tokens are never exposed in JSON responses.
+* Successful Message-IDs are recorded transactionally with the message in a
+  durable, globally unique ledger, including concurrent or differently encoded
+  deliveries. Rejected/failed deliveries do not reserve an ID and may be retried,
+  even with identical raw content. The ledger survives raw-mail incineration.
+
+Raw messages use Active Storage: local disk under `storage/` normally, and
+`tmp/storage/` in tests. Persist this directory across deployments or configure a
+shared Active Storage service when running multiple application hosts. Action
+Mailbox routing uses Active Job; production deployments should configure a
+persistent queue backend and workers for routing and delayed raw-mail
+incineration (Rails defaults to 30 days). Tests route mail **inline before the
+relay response**, so no separate worker is needed. Delayed incineration is not
+run inline. Verification and staff reply mail use the existing environment's
+Action Mailer delivery settings (SMTP in production).
+
+The authenticated SMTP relay is a trust boundary: it must perform appropriate
+sender authentication/spam checks (SPF/DKIM/DMARC) before forwarding mail. The
+application checks requester addresses but cannot itself authenticate an SMTP
+sender from raw RFC822 headers.
+
+## Run
+
+```sh
+bundle install
+export DATABASE_URL='postgresql:///helpdesk_development?host=/var/lib/postgresql'
+bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails server # Puma, port 3000 (or set PORT)
+```
+
+`DATABASE_URL` is required in **every environment**; no database credentials are
+hardcoded. Use a URL appropriate to your PostgreSQL installation.
+
+Configuration:
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` | `support.example.test` | Incoming support and private ticket reply address domain |
+| `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD` | — | Required HTTP Basic password for Action Mailbox relay ingress |
+| `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:3001` | Absolute HTTP(S) frontend URL for email links; its origin is allowed by CORS. A frontend path prefix is supported. |
+| `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` | `support@example.test` | Action Mailer sender |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS` | `localhost` | Production SMTP server |
+| `SMTP_PORT` | `25` | Production SMTP port |
+| `SMTP_DOMAIN` | `localhost` | SMTP HELO domain |
+| `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD` | unset | Optional SMTP credentials (PLAIN authentication with automatic STARTTLS) |
+| `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO` | `true` | Set to `false` only for an appropriate local SMTP relay |
+
+In development, Action Mailer writes outgoing messages to `tmp/mails` so signup
+works without a local SMTP service. Tests use Action Mailer's test delivery
+adapter. Production uses SMTP; configure a real relay and run behind HTTPS.
+Set `SECRET_KEY_BASE` in production (for example, generate it with
+`bin/rails secret`). Encrypted Rails credentials are not required.
+Emails are delivered synchronously after database changes commit. If delivery
+fails, users can request another link; already-created accounts are preserved.
+
+## API
+
+After signup, select the business with `X-Helpdesk-Account: acme`, including on
+public verification, recovery, invitation, and portal requests. The header is a **business
+selector, not authentication**. Authenticated requests must also send
+`Authorization: Bearer TOKEN`. Tokens cannot be used in another business.
+
+### Signup
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/account/create
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"account":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme","admin":{"name":"Jo","email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}}
+```
+
+Returns **201**:
+
+```json
+{
+  "account": {
+    "id": 1,
+    "name": "Acme",
+    "subdomain": "acme",
+    "owner_id": 1,
+    "cancelled_at": null,
+    "created_at": "2026-10-08T12:00:00.000Z",
+    "updated_at": "2026-10-08T12:00:00.000Z"
+  },
+  "user": {
+    "id": 1,
+    "account_id": 1,
+    "name": "Jo",
+    "email": "jo@acme.test",
+    "role": "admin",
+    "verified": false,
+    "suspended": false,
+    "created_at": "2026-10-08T12:00:00.000Z",
+    "updated_at": "2026-10-08T12:00:00.000Z"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The first admin is the account owner. Signup is atomic and sends a verification
+email but does not issue a session. Subdomains and emails are stripped and
+lowercased. Subdomains must be 1–63 ASCII letters/digits, with optional internal
+hyphens. Names are required. Passwords must have at least 8 characters and at
+most 72 bytes (bcrypt's limit). Email uniqueness is per business, enforced by
+PostgreSQL as well as Rails.
+
+### Endpoints
+
+The following paths are relative to `/api/v1`:
+
+| Method / path | JSON body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /registrations` | `{"user":{"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test","password":"my-customer-password"}}` | **201** `{"user":{...},"account":{...}}`, public customer signup; sends verification email, no session |
+| `GET /portal` | — | **200** `{"portal":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme"}}`, public |
+| `POST /email_verifications` | `{"email_verification":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | **202**, generic message |
+| `POST /email_verifications/confirm` | `{"email_verification":{"token":"TOKEN"}}` | **200** `{"user":{...}}`, now verified |
+| `POST /sessions` | `{"session":{"email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}` | **201** `{"user":{...},"account":{...},"session":{"token":"TOKEN"}}` |
+| `DELETE /session` | — | **204**, revokes this session only |
+| `GET /account` | — | **200** `{"account":{...}}`, authenticated |
+| `GET /me` | — | **200** `{"user":{...}}`, authenticated |
+| `PATCH /me` | `{"user":{"name":"Jo Smith"}}` | **200** `{"user":{...}}`, authenticated; only `name` is editable |
+| `PUT /me/password` | `{"password_change":{"current_password":"my-first-password","password":"my-new-password"}}` | **204**, authenticated; revokes all this user's sessions |
+| `POST /password_resets` | `{"password_reset":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | **202**, generic message |
+| `POST /password_resets/confirm` | `{"password_reset":{"token":"TOKEN","password":"my-new-password"}}` | **204**; revokes all this user's sessions |
+| `POST /account/cancel` | — | **200** `{"account":{...}}`, owner only; sets `cancelled_at` and revokes all business sessions |
+| `POST /account/reactivate` | — | **200** `{"account":{...}}`, owner only; clears `cancelled_at` within 90 days |
+
+Verification links are `${HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL}/verify-email?account=acme&token=TOKEN`
+and expire after **24 hours**. Reset links use `/reset-password` with the same
+query parameters and expire after **1 hour**. The frontend submits the token
+in the appropriate JSON envelope with the account header. Confirmation never
+requires a bearer token. Tokens are single-use, business-scoped, and purpose-
+specific; requesting another link replaces the previous token of that type.
+Password recovery does not itself mark an unverified user as verified.
+
+Sessions expire after **30 days**. Passwords use bcrypt; bearer and email tokens
+are random 256-bit values whose SHA-256 digests, not raw values, are stored in
+the database. Password changes also invalidate pending password-reset links.
+Secret fields are never serialized in record responses.
+
+CORS allows only the configured frontend origin, including preflight requests
+with `Content-Type`, `Authorization`, and `X-Helpdesk-Account` headers.
+
+### Team invitations and people management
+
+All paths below are relative to `/api/v1`. Management requires an active
+business and a verified, unsuspended **admin** bearer token. Agents can work on
+tickets and select staff for assignments, but cannot manage users or invitations.
+
+| Method / path | JSON body / query | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /invitations` | `{"invitation":{"email":"sam@example.test","role":"agent"}}` | **201** `{"invitation":{...}}`; emails the frontend acceptance link |
+| `GET /invitations` | Optional `status=pending\|accepted\|revoked\|expired` | **200** `{"invitations":[...],...pagination}`, ID ascending |
+| `GET /invitations/:id` | — | **200** `{"invitation":{...}}` |
+| `POST /invitations/:id/resend` | — | **200** `{"invitation":{...}}`; replaces the token and expiry, then emails a new link |
+| `DELETE /invitations/:id` | — | **204**; revokes the invitation, retaining its history |
+| `POST /invitation_previews` | `{"invitation_preview":{"token":"TOKEN"}}` | **200** `{"invitation_preview":{"email":"sam@example.test","role":"agent","business_name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme","expires_at":"..."}}`; public, no bearer token |
+| `POST /invitation_acceptances` | `{"invitation_acceptance":{"token":"TOKEN","name":"Sam","password":"my-team-password"}}` | **201** `{"user":{...},"account":{...}}`; public, no bearer token |
+| `GET /users` | Optional `role` and `suspended=true\|false` filters | **200** `{"users":[...],...pagination}`, ID ascending |
+| `GET /users/:id` | — | **200** `{"user":{...}}` |
+| `PATCH /users/:id` | `{"user":{"name":"Sam","role":"agent","suspended":true}}` (any supported fields) | **200** `{"user":{...}}` |
+| `GET /staff` | Pagination | **200** `{"staff":[...],...pagination}`; verified, unsuspended staff, ID ascending; admins and agents can use this endpoint |
+
+Invitation roles are restricted to `admin` and `agent`. Invitations expose ID,
+account ID, email, role, status, expiry, acceptance/revocation timestamps, and
+creation/update timestamps, but never raw tokens or token digests. Links use
+`${HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL}/accept-invitation?account=acme&token=TOKEN` and expire
+in **7 days**. The frontend uses the account query parameter to send
+`X-Helpdesk-Account` on preview and acceptance requests. Neither endpoint works
+with expired, revoked, already-accepted, wrong-business, or replaced tokens.
+
+Pending invitations reserve their normalized email within the business.
+Another invitation or customer registration cannot claim it, and existing
+users cannot be invited. Revocation or expiry releases that reservation.
+Pending or expired invitations can be resent unless another pending invitation
+or existing user has since claimed the email. Accepted and revoked invitations
+cannot be resent; accepted invitations cannot be revoked (suspend the user
+instead). Expiration is checked on every token use and reflected in lists and
+status filters without requiring a scheduled job.
+
+Acceptance is atomic and single-use. It creates the invited email and role,
+marks the user verified, and sends **no verification email**. The user can then
+sign in through `POST /sessions`; acceptance itself does not create a session.
+Client-supplied email, role, business, or verification state cannot override the
+invitation.
+
+Every user response includes the boolean `suspended`. Suspension immediately
+revokes all sessions, blocks login, and clears the user's ticket assignments.
+Unsuspending does not restore old sessions or assignments. Suspending or
+demoting the last active, verified admin returns **422**. People management
+operations are serialized per business to enforce this even under concurrent
+requests. `/me` remains the authenticated self-service profile endpoint; it
+cannot change role or suspension. Invalid filter values return **400**.
+
+### Groups and memberships
+
+Groups organize work within a business; they do **not** restrict ticket visibility.
+All staff (`admin`, `agent`, and legacy `member`) can browse groups and memberships.
+Only admins can create, rename, delete, or select defaults. These routes require
+`X-Helpdesk-Account` and a bearer token, like the other staff endpoints.
+
+| Method / path | JSON body or query | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /groups` | `{"group":{"name":"Billing"}}` | **201** `{"group":{...}}` |
+| `GET /groups` | Pagination | **200** `{"groups":[...],...pagination}`; ID ascending |
+| `GET /groups/:id` | — | **200** `{"group":{...}}` |
+| `PATCH /groups/:id` | `{"group":{"name":"Technical Support"}}` | **200** `{"group":{...}}` |
+| `DELETE /groups/:id` | — | **204** |
+| `POST /group_memberships` | `{"group_membership":{"group_id":20,"user_id":12,"default":true}}` | **201** `{"group_membership":{...}}` |
+| `GET /group_memberships` | Optional positive `group_id` and `user_id` filters, pagination | **200** `{"group_memberships":[...],...pagination}`; ID ascending |
+| `GET /group_memberships/:id` | — | **200** `{"group_membership":{...}}` |
+| `DELETE /group_memberships/:id` | — | **204** |
+| `PUT /group_memberships/:id/make_default` | — | **200** `{"group_membership":{...}}` |
+
+Paths above are relative to `/api/v1`. Group responses include `id`, `account_id`,
+`name`, and timestamps. Names are trimmed, required, at most 255 characters,
+and unique within a business (case-insensitively). Membership responses include
+`id`, `account_id`, `group_id`, `user_id`, the boolean `default`, and timestamps.
+Each staff member can join multiple groups, but only once per group. Both the
+group and staff member must belong to the selected business.
+
+The first membership automatically becomes the default, even if `default` is
+omitted or false. Creating a membership with `default:true` or using
+`make_default` replaces the previous default. Removing a default membership
+(including through group deletion) chooses the remaining membership with the
+lowest ID. Staff with no memberships have no default. Suspension keeps all
+memberships and the default for later; suspended staff can still have their
+memberships managed by admins.
+
+Staff ticket responses include nullable `group_id` and `group`:
+
+```json
+{"group_id":20,"group":{"id":20,"name":"Billing"}}
+```
+
+Staff can set `group_id` in `PATCH /tickets/:id`, alone or together with other
+fields. A ticket assignee must be active staff and belong to its group. Assigning
+staff to an ungrouped ticket adopts their default group, if present; otherwise
+the ticket stays ungrouped. Setting a new group alone retains a compatible
+assignee, but clears one who is not a member. An explicitly supplied incompatible
+assignee returns **422**, and the entire update rolls back. Clearing
+`assignee_id` leaves the group in place. Explicitly setting `group_id:null`
+clears both group and assignee, even if an assignee is supplied in the same body.
+Deleting a membership clears that user's assignments in that group, without
+changing the ticket's group. Deleting a group clears both group and assignee on
+its tickets. Default changes do not reroute existing tickets.
+
+Groups and memberships never grant access to tickets in another business.
+Customers cannot browse groups or memberships, change ticket routing, or see
+internal group metadata in `/requests` responses. Group-only changes and
+routing cleanup do not change customer-visible activity.
+
+### Portal requests and staff tickets
+
+Customers register in an **active business** using `POST /registrations` and
+`X-Helpdesk-Account`. Registrations always create the `customer` role; clients
+cannot choose a role, business, or verification state. Customers use the same
+verification/resend, login/logout, `/me`, password change, and password recovery
+endpoints as other users. They must verify their email before signing in.
+Admins (including the owner) and agents are staff. The legacy `member` role
+also retains staff access. Only admins can manage people.
+
+All conversation endpoints require the business header and a verified user's
+bearer token. Customer routes are restricted to their own requests; staff routes
+are restricted to tickets belonging to the selected business. Other customers'
+request IDs and other businesses' ticket IDs return **404**. Customers cannot
+use staff routes, and staff cannot use customer routes (**403**).
+
+| Method / path | JSON body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /requests` | `{"request":{"subject":"Cannot download my receipt","comment":{"body":"The download button does nothing."}}}` | **201** `{"request":{...}}`; creates an open request and its first public comment atomically |
+| `GET /requests` | — | **200** `{"requests":[...],...pagination}`; current customer's requests |
+| `GET /requests/:id` | — | **200** `{"request":{...}}` |
+| `PATCH /requests/:id` | `{"request":{"comment":{"body":"It works now."},"status":"solved"}}` | **200** `{"request":{...}}` |
+| `GET /requests/:id/comments` | — | **200** `{"comments":[...],...pagination}`; public comments only |
+| `GET /tickets` | — | **200** `{"tickets":[...],...pagination}`; all tickets in the business, staff only |
+| `GET /tickets/:id` | — | **200** `{"ticket":{...}}`, staff only |
+| `PATCH /tickets/:id` | `{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Please try again now."},"status":"pending","priority":"high"}}` | **200** `{"ticket":{...}}`, staff only |
+| `GET /tickets/:id/comments` | — | **200** `{"comments":[...],...pagination}`; all comments, staff only |
+| `POST /tickets/:id/merge` | `{"merge":{"target_id":57}}` | **200** `{"source":{...},"target":{...}}`, staff only |
+
+Request records contain `id`, `subject`, `status`, `requester_id`, `merged_into_id`, `created_at`,
+and `updated_at`. Ticket records contain these same attributes plus
+`requester: {"id":10,"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test"}`,
+`group_id` (nullable), `group: {"id":20,"name":"Billing"}` (or null),
+`assignee_id` (nullable), `assignee: {"id":12,"name":"Sam"}` (or null), and
+`priority` (`normal` or `high`, defaulting to `normal`). Group, assignment, and priority
+metadata are never included in customer request responses.
+Comments contain `id`, `body`, `author: {"id":10,"name":"Avery"}`, `public`
+(a boolean), and `created_at`. Passwords and tokens are never included.
+Subjects are required, stripped, and limited to 255 characters; comment bodies
+must not be blank. Creation always uses the signed-in customer as requester
+and author, sets status to `open`, and requires the first comment.
+
+Staff updates accept any combination of comment, status, priority, `group_id`, and `assignee_id`.
+Assignment accepts an active, verified staff user in the same business, or
+`null` to clear it. Invalid assignments return **422** and roll back the whole
+update. Customers cannot set assignments. Customer updates accept a comment,
+a status, or both. Staff can set `open`, `pending`, `solved`, or `closed`.
+Closed tickets are read-only; a staff comment on its own does **not** change status. Customers
+can explicitly set only `solved`. A customer reply reopens a pending or solved
+request unless that same update sets `solved`. Customer comments are always
+public. Staff comments default to public, but staff may set
+`"public":false` for an internal note, which is hidden from customer responses
+and pagination counts. Invalid comments or statuses roll back the entire update.
+Replies are posted only through the resource's **PATCH** endpoint, not by
+posting to `/comments`. Portal-only conversations do **not** send email;
+public staff replies on email-origin tickets use the email behavior described above.
+
+Staff can update `priority` on its own or together with status, assignment,
+and a comment. Null or unsupported priorities return **422**; malformed types
+(such as arrays, objects, numbers, or booleans) return **400**. All changes are atomic. Customer-supplied priority is
+ignored on request creation and updates, just like assignment metadata.
+
+#### Merging duplicate tickets
+
+Staff can merge a ticket into a different, non-closed ticket belonging to the
+**same customer in the same business**:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/tickets/42/merge
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"merge":{"target_id":57}}
+```
+
+The operation atomically closes the source, sets its `merged_into_id` to the
+target's ID, and creates one explanatory note on each ticket authored by the
+staff member. Both full staff ticket records are returned as `source` and `target`.
+Old comments stay on their original ticket. The target's status, priority,
+assignment, group, and email identity are unchanged; notes update activity
+according to their visibility.
+
+Notes default to private. Either note can independently override the default
+text and/or visibility:
+
+```json
+{"merge":{"target_id":57,"source_comment":{"body":"Continuing in ticket 57.","public":true},"target_comment":{"body":"Duplicate checked.","public":false}}}
+```
+
+An omitted body uses the default explanatory text. A supplied body must be a
+nonblank string, and `public` must be a JSON boolean. **Merge notes never send
+email**, even when public and on an email-origin ticket. Regular public staff
+replies on the surviving ticket still send email as usual.
+
+`merged_into_id` is present in both staff and customer records, and is `null`
+when unmerged. Closed tickets and their old visible comments remain readable;
+both `/tickets?status=closed` and `/requests?status=closed` are supported.
+All staff/customer PATCH updates to closed tickets return **422**
+(`ticket_closed`). Incoming email replies to closed tickets are bounced: they
+do not reopen the source, create another ticket, or forward to the target.
+Staff suspension/demotion, membership removal, and group deletion still clear
+invalid assignments/routing on closed tickets.
+
+Self-merges, different-customer merges, and closed participants return **422**;
+missing or other-business IDs return **404**. Malformed payloads return **400**.
+A failed operation leaves both tickets and their comments unchanged. Repeating
+a merge is rejected without adding more notes. If ticket 57 later merges into
+ticket 63, ticket 42 continues pointing to 57: links and notes preserve the
+original history rather than being rewritten.
+
+Ticket lists accept these optional filters, combined with **AND**:
+
+| Query parameter | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `status` | `open`, `pending`, `solved`, or `closed` |
+| `assignee_id` | Positive user ID, or `unassigned` for no assignee |
+| `group_id` | Positive group ID, or `unassigned` for no group |
+| `requester_id` | Positive user ID |
+| `priority` | `normal` or `high` |
+| `q` | Case-insensitive literal substring in the subject or any **public** comment |
+
+For example:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?status=open&assignee_id=unassigned&priority=high&q=receipt
+```
+
+Search never matches private notes. `%`, `_`, backslashes, and SQL-like text
+are treated literally, not as patterns or SQL. An empty `q` adds no search
+restriction. Invalid filters return **400**. Well-formed people IDs that do not
+match a ticket in the selected business return an empty list.
+Customers can filter `/requests` by `status` only; staff-only filters are
+ignored, and results always remain restricted to their own requests. Filters
+are applied before pagination, and multiple matching comments never duplicate
+a ticket or inflate `count`.
+
+Legacy page-number pagination on every list (including comments) accepts
+positive integer `page` and `per_page`,
+defaulting to **1** and **25**. Invalid pagination returns **400**. Metadata is
+at the top level alongside the plural resource name:
+
+```json
+{
+  "requests": [],
+  "count": 0,
+  "page": 1,
+  "per_page": 25,
+  "next_page": null,
+  "previous_page": null
+}
+```
+
+`count` is the total number of visible matching records, not just the current
+page's size. Missing adjacent pages are `null`; pages beyond the last return
+an empty list. Requests and tickets are ordered by most recently updated first,
+with descending ID as a tie-breaker. Public replies and status changes update customer-visible activity. Private
+notes, group, assignment or priority changes, and routing/suspension cleanup update only internal ticket
+activity: they do not change a request's `updated_at`, status, visible comments,
+pagination counts, or position in the customer request list. Staff ticket lists
+include internal activity. Comments are ordered oldest first, with ascending ID
+as a tie-breaker.
+
+### Cursor pagination
+
+All nine collection routes also support cursor pagination: `/tickets`, `/requests`,
+both conversation comment routes, `/users`, `/staff`, `/invitations`, `/groups`,
+and `/group_memberships`. Opt in using nested `page` parameters instead of the
+legacy scalar `page`/`per_page` parameters:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+```
+
+The record objects and visibility rules are unchanged. Cursor responses replace
+page-number/count metadata with:
+
+```json
+{
+  "tickets": [],
+  "meta": {
+    "has_more": false,
+    "after_cursor": null,
+    "before_cursor": null
+  },
+  "links": { "next": null, "prev": null }
+}
+```
+
+A nonempty page always has `before_cursor` for its **first** record and
+`after_cursor` for its **last** record, including at the beginning/end of the
+list. Empty pages have null cursors and links. `links.next` requests records after
+the last record; `links.prev` requests records before the first record. Missing
+adjacent pages have null links. Links are relative API URLs and preserve filters
+and page size; send the same account and authentication headers when following
+them. Alternatively, send `page[after]=<after_cursor>` or
+`page[before]=<before_cursor>` with `page[size]` and the original filters.
+`has_more` indicates more records **in the requested direction** (forward on an
+initial/after request, backward on a before request). Results always retain their
+normal display order, even when browsing backward.
+
+The cursor page size defaults to **25** and accepts positive integers through
+**100**. It may change between requests. Empty/malformed cursors, unknown nested
+page parameters, simultaneous `after` and `before`, and mixing cursor parameters
+with `per_page` return **400** `invalid_request`. Cursors are signed and bound to
+the account, authenticated user, collection/parent, applied filters, and ordering;
+reuse on a different list or with different filters returns **400**. Treat cursors
+as opaque and URL-encode them when constructing requests yourself.
+
+Cursor tickets and requests use **creation time descending, ID descending**;
+comments use **creation time ascending, ID ascending**; people, staff,
+invitations, groups, and memberships use **ID ascending**. These immutable keys
+prevent replies, private notes, routing changes, or edits from moving a record
+across the cursor. The ID tie-breaker preserves timestamp precision and handles
+records created at the same instant. Each traversal carries an insertion
+high-water mark, excluding later inserts (including backdated records) until a
+fresh first request. Deleted cursor anchors do not invalidate navigation.
+
+This is a live, bounded traversal, **not** a frozen database snapshot. Records
+can be deleted or stop matching filters/visibility, and returned fields reflect
+current values; permissions are checked on every request. Start a fresh traversal
+to see new records. Cursor queries fetch at most `size + 1` records, use indexed
+keyset comparisons, and perform neither total counts nor offsets, so seeking
+deeper does not require walking all preceding pages. Legacy page-number requests
+(including requests without nested `page`) retain their existing count metadata
+and activity-based ticket/request ordering.
+
+### Cancellation and cleanup
+
+Cancellation blocks portal, account, and ordinary profile access. Existing
+sessions, including the owner's, are revoked. During the 90-day grace period,
+a verified owner can sign in again and use that new session to reactivate or
+sign out. Other users cannot sign in while cancelled. The owner can still
+recover a forgotten password. Cancelling again does not restart the clock.
+At 90 days, login and reactivation are refused even if cleanup has not yet run.
+
+Schedule this task (for example, daily via cron) using your production
+`DATABASE_URL`, `RAILS_ENV`, and mail/application environment:
+
+```sh
+RAILS_ENV=production bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+```
+
+It permanently destroys accounts cancelled for **at least 90 days**, all their
+users and tokens, all sessions, invitations, and all tickets and comments. Active, reactivated, and recently cancelled
+accounts are retained. Cleanup rechecks eligibility under a database lock to
+avoid deleting an account concurrently reactivated by its owner.
+
+### Errors
+
+Errors use JSON:
+
+```json
+{"error":{"code":"validation_failed","message":"Validation failed.","details":{"email":["is invalid"]}}}
+```
+
+`details` is optional. Missing business selection or malformed envelopes/JSON
+return **400**. Unknown businesses return **404**. Missing/invalid/expired
+sessions and incorrect login credentials return **401**. Unverified login,
+cancelled business access, and non-owner account operations return **403**.
+Invalid/expired/used email tokens and failed validations return **422**.
+Resend/recovery requests return the same **202** response whether or not the
+email is eligible, without disclosing user existence.
+
+## Verify
+
+**Use a separate disposable test database**: Rails uses `DATABASE_URL` as-is,
+including in the test environment.
+
+```sh
+export DATABASE_URL='postgresql:///helpdesk_test?host=/var/lib/postgresql'
+RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails test
+bin/rubocop
+bin/brakeman --no-pager
+# Or run all checks (including dependency audit):
+RAILS_ENV=test bin/ci
+```
+
+Tests exercise JSON responses, atomic signup, mail links and custom sender,
+CORS, verification and recovery, expiring/replaced tokens, tenant isolation,
+profile updates, session revocation, owner permissions, cancellation and the
+90-day deletion boundary, customer registration and recovery, conversation
+visibility and isolation, status transitions, atomic replies, ordering, and
+pagination. The original signup reproduction is
+`test/integration/signup_test.rb`.
+
+The portal conversation reproduction is `test/integration/portal_conversations_test.rb`.
+Team management and customer-visibility regression tests are in
+`test/integration/staff_management_test.rb`. Real concurrent PostgreSQL request
+tests are in `test/integration/staff_concurrency_test.rb`.
+
+Priority and filter regressions are in `test/integration/ticket_filters_test.rb`.
+
+Group and routing regressions are in `test/integration/groups_api_test.rb`,
+`test/integration/ticket_groups_test.rb`, and `test/models/group_membership_test.rb`.
+Concurrent membership creation, default switches, and routing cleanup are tested
+in `test/integration/group_concurrency_test.rb`.
+
+Merge regressions are in `test/integration/ticket_merges_test.rb` and
+`test/integration/ticket_merge_concurrency_test.rb`, including atomic rollback,
+privacy, email suppression, immutable chains, and merge/reply races.
